244
00:17:17,521 --> 00:17:18,324
the time.

245
00:17:18,325 --> 00:17:25,920
By the 13th century, European craftsmen were constructing mechanical monastery clocks

246
00:17:25,921 --> 00:17:31,640
that used large weights and gears to keep time, and bells to toll the hour.

247
00:17:36,100 --> 00:17:42,180
Originally, the alarm clock was invented to mark religious observance, to honor different

248
00:17:42,181 --> 00:17:46,980
times of the day when different deities, saints, and rituals needed to be observed.

249
00:17:47,520 --> 00:17:53,840
And then that was extrapolated out into being able to bring the population to worship

250
00:17:53,841 --> 00:18:01,880
and go to the fields and work, when to come home, when to pray, when to get up, when to eat.

251
00:18:03,460 --> 00:18:08,740
In 16th century Europe, a wound spring mechanism helped make timekeeping more portable.

252
00:18:11,200 --> 00:18:17,300
No longer encumbered by weights, chamber clocks became the predecessors of modern bedroom alarm clocks.

253
00:18:17,520 --> 00:18:26,840
The West Coast Watch and Clock Museum in Bellingham, Washington, is home to an impressive collection

254
00:18:26,841 --> 00:18:30,740
of historic timekeepers.

255
00:18:30,741 --> 00:18:35,020
This is late 16th century going into the 17th century.

256
00:18:35,021 --> 00:18:39,280
First you bought your basic clock, which was very expensive to start with.

257
00:18:39,281 --> 00:18:43,600
And then if you were affluent enough, you bought your separate alarm mechanism.

258
00:18:43,601 --> 00:18:46,660
And this functions by taking the small pointer here,

259
00:18:46,661 --> 00:18:49,020
putting it on top of your clock,

260
00:18:49,021 --> 00:18:53,440
and then as the hand progresses against the pointer,

261
00:18:55,680 --> 00:18:56,680
the alarm will sound.

262
00:18:59,020 --> 00:19:01,140
As demand for household alarm clocks grew

263
00:19:01,141 --> 00:19:03,600
throughout the 18th and 19th centuries,

264
00:19:03,601 --> 00:19:06,660
clockmakers began crafting timepieces with form,

265
00:19:06,661 --> 00:19:07,720
as well as function.

266
00:19:07,721 --> 00:19:17,260
Whimsical animated alarm features were fun, but not always so safe.

267
00:19:18,460 --> 00:19:22,780
This was made sometime in the late 1850s, early 1860s.

268
00:19:23,120 --> 00:19:26,960
When it functions, the match will light an oil lamp.

269
00:19:27,900 --> 00:19:28,960
Did you catch that?

270
00:19:28,961 --> 00:19:41,480
and quite frequently the match would fly out of the holder fly across the bedroom set the bed

271
00:19:41,481 --> 00:19:46,580
curtains on fire or the sleeve of the user and the entire house would burn down so there's very few

272
00:19:46,581 --> 00:19:53,520
examples of this intact today that are all original then at the turn of the 20th century

273
00:19:53,521 --> 00:19:59,100
came a model some still acknowledge as the quintessential yet reviled alarm clock design.

274
00:20:01,020 --> 00:20:03,120
The wind-up bell alarm clock.

275
00:20:06,960 --> 00:20:12,500
When you wind the alarm, you tighten the spring, which is kept from unwinding by a release arm.

276
00:20:13,980 --> 00:20:20,540
A gear with a ramped face holds the release in place, and a slotted-faced gear fits against the ramp.

277
00:20:20,541 --> 00:20:27,540
When it reaches the appointed hour, the hold on the release arm is disengaged, freeing

278
00:20:27,541 --> 00:20:32,680
the hammer, allowing the spring to unwind.

279
00:20:32,681 --> 00:20:36,900
The hammer is attached to an escapement, which meters the hammer's action, causing it to

280
00:20:36,901 --> 00:20:45,580
rock back and forth, repeatedly striking the alarm bells.

281
00:20:45,581 --> 00:20:49,580
The 1950s brought us a function that would revolutionize waking up as we knew it.

282
00:20:51,100 --> 00:20:53,160
All hail the snooze alarm.

283
00:20:54,720 --> 00:20:56,940
Whether it operates mechanically or electronically,

284
00:20:57,700 --> 00:21:02,080
this magic button gives us a precious reprieve from the day to come.

285
00:21:04,840 --> 00:21:07,840
But alarms are only useful if you're actually sleeping.

286
00:21:08,700 --> 00:21:12,500
If there's trouble in the bedroom, you may be suffering from a sleep disorder,

287
00:21:12,740 --> 00:21:15,500
which could range from frustrating to fatal.

288
00:21:15,580 --> 00:21:20,460
There's a small number of individuals that have chronic insomnia.

289
00:21:20,680 --> 00:21:24,020
Insomnia lasts more than a month, in fact, some of the last years.

290
00:21:24,500 --> 00:21:29,200
And those are the people who really have significant daytime functional impairment.

291
00:21:30,020 --> 00:21:37,100
A more serious condition known as sleep apnea actually causes the sleeper to stop breathing dozens of times during the night.

292
00:21:39,640 --> 00:21:43,540
The throat involuntarily collapses during the night.

293
00:21:43,541 --> 00:21:48,400
and then the breathing cessation will be recovered by loud snorting noise.

294
00:21:49,120 --> 00:21:52,380
They can become restless, just like someone who's being strangled.

295
00:21:53,240 --> 00:21:58,320
In almost all cases, the brain overrides what's happening in the throat and breathing resumes.

296
00:21:59,540 --> 00:22:01,960
But the dangers of apnea don't stop there.

297
00:22:02,880 --> 00:22:08,680
Recent studies have shown that sleep apnea is associated with increased risk of stroke and heart attack.

298
00:22:08,681 --> 00:22:14,120
In fact, there's two to four times risk of stroke and heart attack in these individuals.

299
00:22:15,400 --> 00:22:19,140
At Premier Diagnostic Sleep Clinic in Thousand Oaks, California,

300
00:22:20,700 --> 00:22:24,100
patients must be prepared to spend the night under some serious scrutiny.

301
00:22:26,980 --> 00:22:29,860
These wires will measure brain waves and eye activity

302
00:22:29,861 --> 00:22:34,120
to determine when rapid eye movement occurs, indicating deep sleep.

303
00:22:34,121 --> 00:22:37,800
Leg sensors will gauge limb movement.

304
00:22:38,680 --> 00:22:40,540
Chest belts will measure breathing patterns.

305
00:22:41,500 --> 00:22:45,240
A snore monitor and airflow sensor do what each name suggests.

306
00:22:46,160 --> 00:22:50,360
And a pulse oximeter will pick up his heart rate and blood oxygen level.

307
00:22:52,580 --> 00:22:55,500
After lights out, the experts watch and wait.

308
00:22:57,220 --> 00:23:01,080
We have an actual digital picture of the patient all night long

309
00:23:01,081 --> 00:23:05,500
that we can coordinate certain events with the actual activity of the patient's body.

310
00:23:08,200 --> 00:23:13,420
So this is exactly what REM sleep is going to look like, the real fast flickering of the eyes.

311
00:23:19,260 --> 00:23:24,160
These are multiple snorts, which are actually typical of coming out of an apnea event.

312
00:23:24,840 --> 00:23:30,300
After the sleep study has been completed, the digital information that is recorded in the computer

313
00:23:30,301 --> 00:23:31,960
is scored by a technician.

314
00:23:34,080 --> 00:23:37,120
Sleep apnea treatment can include wearing a dental device

315
00:23:37,121 --> 00:23:38,860
or a CPAP mask

316
00:23:38,861 --> 00:23:41,300
or in extreme cases,

317
00:23:42,320 --> 00:23:43,020
surgery.

318
00:23:44,500 --> 00:23:47,740
Just when you're ready to crawl into bed and catch some Zs,

319
00:23:48,200 --> 00:23:52,460
these beastly little bedfellows might be crawling in there with you.

320
00:23:53,200 --> 00:23:54,700
The bedbugs are back.

321
00:23:54,701 --> 00:23:55,724
In 1942, U.S.

322
00:23:55,725 --> 00:24:03,840
household alarm clock production halted because factories and materials were needed for the war effort.

323
00:24:04,680 --> 00:24:12,920
The resulting epidemic of worker tardiness prompted the government to approve limited production of these clocks using non-war essential materials.

324
00:24:13,840 --> 00:24:16,360
Bedroom Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

325
00:24:18,920 --> 00:24:22,240
We now return to Bedroom Tech on Modern Marvels.

326
00:24:24,700 --> 00:24:28,960
Hamaker Schlemmer, New York's source for unique gadgets since 1848,

327
00:24:29,480 --> 00:24:34,020
has a few solutions for those who can't sleep with all that pesky peace and quiet.

328
00:24:36,200 --> 00:24:39,500
This is the authentic sound Oasis machine.

329
00:24:39,940 --> 00:24:45,040
The sounds that are featured here are actually recorded at the source.

330
00:24:45,420 --> 00:24:47,700
The first sound is ocean surf.

331
00:24:50,460 --> 00:24:53,060
The second sound we'll listen to here is a stream.

332
00:24:54,700 --> 00:24:58,700
The third sound we'll listen to here is the rain.

333
00:24:58,701 --> 00:25:02,700
It was raining one day or evening and someone recorded it there.

334
00:25:02,701 --> 00:25:06,700
The fourth sound is white noise.

335
00:25:10,700 --> 00:25:14,700
The fifth sound is summer night.

336
00:25:14,701 --> 00:25:18,700
The final sound that we can listen to here is the wind.

337
00:25:18,701 --> 00:25:28,000
To maintain fidelity, the audio is stored digitally on a sound card embedded in the machine.

338
00:25:31,080 --> 00:25:34,780
Slightly less sophisticated is the original sound sleep generator.

339
00:25:37,180 --> 00:25:42,517
What it does is makes a white noise sound that will soothe you to sleep.

340
00:25:42,518 --> 00:25:44,000
It masks all outside

341
00:25:44,001 --> 00:25:47,240
of noises that may bother you.

342
00:25:47,241 --> 00:25:51,560
This noise is made by an internal fan that continuously just

343
00:25:51,561 --> 00:25:54,700
turns while the machine is on.

344
00:25:54,701 --> 00:25:57,480
We have customers that come in, and if we don't have it

345
00:25:57,481 --> 00:25:59,440
in stock, they will freak out on us.

346
00:25:59,441 --> 00:26:02,440
They need this to sleep.

347
00:26:02,441 --> 00:26:04,600
Sounds to replace other sounds.

348
00:26:08,440 --> 00:26:10,660
When it comes to getting a sound sleep,

349
00:26:10,661 --> 00:26:12,900
we'll do whatever works.

350
00:26:12,901 --> 00:26:17,940
We'll even share our beds with an electrical appliance.

351
00:26:17,941 --> 00:26:19,940
The first electric blankets produced

352
00:26:19,941 --> 00:26:22,300
in the early 20th century operated

353
00:26:22,301 --> 00:26:26,380
on 120 volts of alternating current.

354
00:26:26,381 --> 00:26:28,180
Faulty or broken wiring could cause

355
00:26:28,181 --> 00:26:33,420
painful, even fatal, electric shock or hot spots that

356
00:26:33,421 --> 00:26:35,800
could lead to fire.

357
00:26:35,801 --> 00:26:38,780
Throw in flammable bedding and a sleeping user,

358
00:26:38,781 --> 00:26:43,080
and you've got a hotbed of danger.

359
00:26:43,081 --> 00:26:44,780
Today's low voltage electric blanket

360
00:26:44,781 --> 00:26:48,000
is comprised of a polyester shell exterior encasing

361
00:26:48,001 --> 00:26:50,820
a copper wire grid.

362
00:26:50,821 --> 00:26:53,600
A flexible PVC coating insulates the grid,

363
00:26:53,601 --> 00:26:55,460
preventing the wiring from being compromised.

364
00:26:59,080 --> 00:27:02,960
The blanket's transformer converts the 120 volts AC

365
00:27:02,961 --> 00:27:06,340
into 18 volts DC, which would be hardly enough

366
00:27:06,341 --> 00:27:09,240
to tingle the skin.

367
00:27:09,241 --> 00:27:11,740
This product is called the safest hidden wires

368
00:27:11,741 --> 00:27:12,900
electric blanket.

369
00:27:12,901 --> 00:27:15,680
Now the best feature of this is that you

370
00:27:15,681 --> 00:27:17,920
can disconnect the wire.

371
00:27:17,921 --> 00:27:22,000
And the wires hidden in here are insulated.

372
00:27:22,001 --> 00:27:24,460
So this goes in the washer and the dryer.

373
00:27:24,461 --> 00:27:27,520
And then it goes right back on the bed.

374
00:27:27,521 --> 00:27:30,860
Bedroom gadgets from noise makers to heat generators

375
00:27:30,861 --> 00:27:35,960
are creature comforts that help us hit the proverbial hay.

376
00:27:35,961 --> 00:27:38,360
Eons ago, when the hay was actual hay,

377
00:27:39,540 --> 00:27:41,740
early man took a cue from his animal counterparts

378
00:27:41,741 --> 00:27:44,840
and built nests on communal cave floors.

379
00:27:46,680 --> 00:27:52,000
Neolithic people actually slept on piles of leaves

380
00:27:52,001 --> 00:27:54,300
with animal fur scattered on top.

381
00:27:54,840 --> 00:27:56,980
And that was really the origin of the first bed.

382
00:27:57,760 --> 00:27:59,920
People began sleeping on beds

383
00:27:59,921 --> 00:28:03,320
because they were looking for more comfortable sleep surfaces.

384
00:28:03,321 --> 00:28:08,420
and they were trying to get off the ground itself and get away from bugs or vermin.

385
00:28:09,340 --> 00:28:15,180
The ancient Egyptians were the earliest people known to have elevated sleeping platforms and private bedrooms.

386
00:28:17,800 --> 00:28:24,780
The pharaohs actually were sleeping in what you would call a bed around 3400 B.C.

387
00:28:25,100 --> 00:28:30,700
Basically, these were short, narrow pallets that had headrests for their head and neck,

388
00:28:30,701 --> 00:28:35,340
which actually were in place more to keep their elaborate hairstyles in place

389
00:28:35,341 --> 00:28:36,740
than providing comforter support.

390
00:28:37,700 --> 00:28:41,620
The ancient Greeks preferred messing up their hair to breaking their necks.

391
00:28:42,600 --> 00:28:46,900
The Greeks were really noted for introducing the pillow,

392
00:28:47,220 --> 00:28:48,580
stuffed with feathers or wool.

393
00:28:50,100 --> 00:28:54,000
Unlike the Greeks who used bedrooms primarily for sleep and sex,

394
00:28:54,420 --> 00:28:58,820
the less modest Romans used theirs as stages for group meals, meetings,

395
00:28:58,821 --> 00:29:01,960
and sometimes the noble task of suicide.

396
00:29:03,960 --> 00:29:07,060
A typical Roman's bed boasted the earliest mattress,

397
00:29:07,900 --> 00:29:10,280
essentially a thin cloth casing or sack

398
00:29:10,281 --> 00:29:13,760
stuck with reeds, hay, wool, or feathers.

399
00:29:15,120 --> 00:29:18,480
When the Roman Empire fell, we go back to the Dark Ages

400
00:29:18,481 --> 00:29:21,260
and basically everyone was sleeping on the ground again.

401
00:29:22,380 --> 00:29:25,480
Then the Renaissance ushered in the resurgence of the bedroom.

402
00:29:25,481 --> 00:29:31,520
European sleeping quarters centered around ornately carved wooden beds

403
00:29:31,521 --> 00:29:39,360
topped with straw or feather mattresses, luxurious coverings, and privacy curtains.

404
00:29:42,040 --> 00:29:47,420
About 5,000 miles east, the Chinese found a way to sleep tight and keep warm

405
00:29:47,421 --> 00:29:51,300
on a kong bed stove.

406
00:29:51,301 --> 00:29:57,780
A kong was a fired clay platform connected to a wood or coal-burning cooking stove.

407
00:29:59,920 --> 00:30:07,880
Hot exhaust from the stove traveled via flue through the cavity of the platform, warming its surface, and then out a chimney.

408
00:30:09,240 --> 00:30:17,180
Even after the fire burned out, the bed retained heat for several hours, making the kong an ideal place for eating and sleeping during winter.

409
00:30:17,181 --> 00:30:24,880
At about nine feet long and six feet wide, the Kong had room for the whole family.

410
00:30:26,340 --> 00:30:30,100
As times progressed, so did the quest for comfort and convenience.

411
00:30:31,100 --> 00:30:34,920
The first innerspring coil was patented in 1865.

412
00:30:36,100 --> 00:30:39,820
During World War II, there was a shortage of steel and iron,

413
00:30:39,980 --> 00:30:44,520
and so there were some great alternative sleep surfaces being developed.

414
00:30:44,521 --> 00:30:49,000
in post-World War II saw the beginning of synthetic rubber mattresses.

415
00:30:49,880 --> 00:30:56,280
And so as time moved on, we went from synthetic rubber mattresses to latex rubber mattresses.

416
00:30:57,420 --> 00:31:02,020
The early 1900s also saw the development of the hideaway, or Murphy bed.

417
00:31:02,920 --> 00:31:07,560
Great for cramped big city apartments, the Murphy bed made the most of limited floor space

418
00:31:07,561 --> 00:31:11,720
by folding up into a closet using a counterbalanced spring mechanism.

419
00:31:14,520 --> 00:31:18,780
Another space saver remains common wherever minimal space must be maximized.

420
00:31:20,940 --> 00:31:26,580
From ships to dormitories, bunk beds eventually became popular in kids' bedrooms

421
00:31:26,581 --> 00:31:29,300
and spawned a colorful military term.

422
00:31:30,480 --> 00:31:34,000
The term hot bunking originates in the early days of submarining,

423
00:31:34,260 --> 00:31:37,240
in the early part of the 20th century and all the way up through World War II.

424
00:31:37,800 --> 00:31:42,980
Submarines were much smaller and there was not space, sufficient space,

425
00:31:42,981 --> 00:31:45,380
to provide a bunk for every crew member.

426
00:31:45,920 --> 00:31:48,000
Junior crew members had to share a bunk

427
00:31:48,001 --> 00:31:49,880
when one individual was on watch.

428
00:31:50,360 --> 00:31:51,960
A second individual would be in the bunk.

429
00:31:52,060 --> 00:31:54,500
When the watch changed, they'd swap places,

430
00:31:54,820 --> 00:31:56,620
and you were climbing into a warm bunk,

431
00:31:57,080 --> 00:31:59,280
sleeping in your own shipmate's smell.

432
00:31:59,960 --> 00:32:01,940
The captain's quarters are definitely different.

433
00:32:03,440 --> 00:32:04,760
It's good to be the king.

434
00:32:06,140 --> 00:32:08,420
Regardless of what type of bed floats your boat,

435
00:32:08,421 --> 00:32:11,580
you may be sharing it with some unwanted companions.

436
00:32:12,980 --> 00:32:17,880
Say hello to that blood-sucking midnight snacker, the bed bug.

437
00:32:21,980 --> 00:32:26,960
Originating in prehistoric caves around the world, where they fed on humans and bats,

438
00:32:28,320 --> 00:32:32,320
bed bugs have been documented pests in the U.S. since the 17th century,

439
00:32:33,460 --> 00:32:38,220
primarily in areas of high turnover, like hotels, dorms, and tenements.

440
00:32:38,221 --> 00:32:45,180
Thanks to the widespread spraying of DDT in the US after World War II,

441
00:32:45,880 --> 00:32:47,500
most of us have never seen a bed bug.

442
00:32:49,700 --> 00:32:54,940
But since the mid-1990s, this mysterious creature of bedtime rhyme fame

443
00:32:54,941 --> 00:32:59,260
has been reprising its reign of terror in a hotel or bedroom near you.

444
00:33:01,880 --> 00:33:04,200
Increased international travel and commerce,

445
00:33:04,360 --> 00:33:06,840
coupled with less effective extermination techniques,

446
00:33:06,841 --> 00:33:09,280
have facilitated the reinfestation,

447
00:33:10,140 --> 00:33:12,300
most notably in large hub cities

448
00:33:12,301 --> 00:33:15,640
like New York and San Francisco.

449
00:33:18,160 --> 00:33:20,040
Bedbugs typically hitch a ride in luggage

450
00:33:20,041 --> 00:33:21,260
and recycled furniture.

451
00:33:22,960 --> 00:33:27,220
They hide in mattress folds, seams, creases, and tufts,

452
00:33:27,221 --> 00:33:29,280
and in wood headboards and furniture.

453
00:33:31,360 --> 00:33:34,100
Sensing our body heat, they emerge to feed at night,

454
00:33:34,101 --> 00:33:38,460
piercing a victim's skin with their sharp beaks and injecting an

455
00:33:38,461 --> 00:33:43,220
anticoagulant saliva to promote blood flow

456
00:33:43,221 --> 00:33:48,580
and then they suck often growing to three times their regular size jacking up

457
00:33:48,581 --> 00:33:54,700
the gross factor they leave behind itchy welts but unlike fleas and mosquitoes

458
00:33:54,701 --> 00:34:02,000
bedbugs aren't known to transmit disease and once the bedbugs have invaded call

459
00:34:02,001 --> 00:34:07,320
the exterminator, because these creepy crawlers are immune to most common household pesticides.

460
00:34:09,000 --> 00:34:13,480
If scratching bed bug bites is preventing you from sleeping tight in your own bed,

461
00:34:13,860 --> 00:34:17,300
how about a high-tech siesta away from home?

462
00:34:21,020 --> 00:34:24,860
Charles Hall constructed the first modern water bed in 1968.

463
00:34:26,040 --> 00:34:31,580
Popularity peaked in 1987 when water beds made up 22% of U.S. mattress sales.

464
00:34:32,000 --> 00:34:35,540
But demand waned in the early 1990s.

465
00:34:37,040 --> 00:34:41,560
Bedroom Tech will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

466
00:34:43,800 --> 00:34:46,840
Return to Bedroom Tech on Modern Marvels.

467
00:34:53,680 --> 00:34:57,400
Thanks to the ever-faithful alarm clock, you've made it to work on time.

468
00:34:57,960 --> 00:35:00,320
But you're still dreaming of going back to bed.

469
00:35:00,321 --> 00:35:05,320
Why not trade in your cubicle for a yellow cab?

470
00:35:05,321 --> 00:35:10,320
It's not a taxi, but an innovative new idea for the sleep deprived.

471
00:35:10,321 --> 00:35:17,320
Yellow is a place where people could come in and out in anywhere between 20 minutes and

472
00:35:17,321 --> 00:35:22,320
one hour and lay and leave basically completely relaxed and recharged.

473
00:35:22,321 --> 00:35:28,320
Located in midtown Manhattan, Yellow hopes it can persuade the city that never sleeps

474
00:35:28,321 --> 00:35:31,320
to take a power nap.

475
00:35:31,321 --> 00:35:33,320
A Harvard University study has shown

476
00:35:33,321 --> 00:35:36,320
that taking a half-hour nap three times a week

477
00:35:36,321 --> 00:35:41,320
could significantly reduce the risk of dying from heart disease.

478
00:35:41,321 --> 00:35:44,320
Need more proof that napping works?

479
00:35:44,321 --> 00:35:50,320
These historical movers and shakers were all daily nappers.

480
00:35:50,321 --> 00:35:54,320
At Yellow, feng shui meets siesta science.

481
00:35:54,321 --> 00:35:56,320
As soon as you stepped in the door,

